WEBVTT

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:02.684
And it shapes what expectations you have,

00:04:02.708 --> 00:04:05.518
how you collaborate,
how you ask for help, how you compete,

00:04:05.542 --> 00:04:06.809
you name it.

00:04:06.833 --> 00:04:10.143
But then, you know, keep going.

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:11.893
(Laughter)

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:13.434
AG: Were you going to say more?

00:04:13.458 --> 00:04:14.851
EP: I can say it afterwards.

00:04:14.875 --> 00:04:18.184
AG: OK. Alright, so one of the things
I've been thinking a lot about

00:04:18.208 --> 00:04:19.934
is power dynamics in relationships.

00:04:19.958 --> 00:04:22.976
And I remember reading
a really fascinating analysis of power.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.393
I think it was by Emerson who argued
that relationships were more stable

00:04:26.417 --> 00:04:28.684
if there was a slight power imbalance,

00:04:28.708 --> 00:04:32.018
because if you knew which person
had a little bit more authority